/** Original policy drafting. Entity elections and facts are captured in the related notes. */
export const SME_POLICY_CONTENT: Record<string,string> = {
  "policyBasisPreparation": "The financial statements are presented in South African Rand. Assets and liabilities are measured on the historical cost basis except for items measured on another basis in the policies below. Comparative information is presented for the preceding period. Transactions are accounted for on the accrual basis, except for cash flow information. Material items are presented separately and assets and liabilities, and income and expenses, are not offset unless the reporting framework requires or permits this. The reporting framework, reporting entity and any changes in policies or presentation are identified in the accompanying disclosures.",
  "policyJudgementsEstimates": "Management makes judgements in applying accounting policies and estimates amounts that cannot be measured directly. Estimates reflect information available at the reporting date and are reviewed when circumstances change. Revisions to estimates are recognised prospectively in the affected periods. The accompanying disclosures identify the judgements with the most significant effect on recognised amounts and the key assumptions and carrying amounts exposed to a significant risk of material adjustment in the next financial year.",
  "policyGoingConcern": "Management assesses the entity’s ability to continue as a going concern using available information about the future, covering at least twelve months from the reporting date. The going concern basis is used unless management intends to liquidate the entity or cease trading, or has no realistic alternative. Material uncertainties that may cast significant doubt on continued operation, and significant judgements in reaching the assessment, are disclosed. If that basis is inappropriate, the alternative basis and reasons are disclosed.",
  "policyFinancialInstruments": "Financial assets and financial liabilities are recognised when the entity becomes a party to the contractual provisions of an instrument. Basic debt instruments are initially measured at transaction price, including transaction costs, unless the arrangement constitutes a financing transaction, when the present value of future payments discounted at a market rate for a similar instrument is used, subject to applicable related-party loan exemptions. Basic debt instruments are subsequently measured at amortised cost using the effective interest method, less impairment for assets. Instruments outside the basic-instrument conditions are measured under the relevant fair value requirements. Transaction costs of instruments at fair value through profit or loss are expensed. Financial assets are derecognised when contractual rights expire or qualifying transfers occur; liabilities are derecognised when discharged, cancelled or expired.",
  "policyTradeReceivables": "Trade receivables are initially recognised at the transaction price unless the arrangement includes a financing transaction. Receivables under normal credit terms are subsequently measured at the undiscounted amount expected to be received, less impairment. Longer-term financing balances are measured at amortised cost using the effective interest method. Objective evidence of impairment is assessed at each reporting date, individually for significant balances and collectively for balances with similar credit risk characteristics. Impairment losses and qualifying reversals are recognised in profit or loss.",
  "policyTradePayables": "Trade and other financial payables are recognised when the entity becomes party to the contractual obligation. Amounts under normal credit terms are measured at the undiscounted amount payable. Arrangements constituting financing transactions are initially measured at present value and subsequently at amortised cost using the effective interest method. Liabilities are derecognised only when discharged, cancelled or expired; a liability is not offset against a receivable merely because the counterparty is the same.",
  "policyLoans": "Loans are assessed against the conditions for basic debt instruments. Qualifying loans are initially recognised at transaction price including directly attributable transaction costs, or present value where the terms constitute financing, and subsequently at amortised cost using the effective interest method. Loans receivable are assessed for objective evidence of impairment at each reporting date. Gains and losses are recognised on impairment, derecognition and through amortisation. Contractual repayment rights determine current and non-current presentation; related-party status does not by itself establish recoverability or waive contractual terms.",
  "policyShareholderLoans": "Shareholder and other related-party loans are classified as financial liabilities or equity according to their contractual substance. Basic debt instruments are measured at transaction price or, for financing transactions, present value, subject to any exemption in the selected edition for qualifying loans. Subsequent measurement is at amortised cost using the effective interest method, with impairment assessed for receivables. Interest-free status does not by itself make a loan equity. Current and non-current classification reflects enforceable settlement terms at the reporting date.",
  "policyCash": "Cash comprises cash on hand and demand deposits. Cash equivalents are short-term, highly liquid investments held to meet short-term cash commitments, readily convertible to known amounts of cash and subject to insignificant risk of changes in value; they normally have a maturity of three months or less from acquisition. Bank overdrafts are included in cash and cash equivalents for cash flow purposes only when repayable on demand and forming an integral part of cash management. Other overdrafts are financing liabilities. Restrictions and the reconciliation to the statement of financial position are disclosed.",
  "policyInventories": "Inventories are measured at the lower of cost and estimated selling price less costs to complete and sell. Cost comprises purchase costs, conversion costs and other expenditure incurred in bringing inventory to its present location and condition. Fixed production overheads are allocated on normal capacity; abnormal waste, unnecessary storage, selling costs and unrelated administrative overheads are expensed. The cost formula used for each inventory class is disclosed and is applied consistently to inventories of similar nature and use. Inventory is expensed when the related revenue is recognised. Write-downs and qualifying reversals are recognised in profit or loss.",
  "policyImpairment": "At each reporting date the entity assesses non-financial assets for indicators of impairment. Where indicated, the recoverable amount is estimated as the higher of fair value less costs to sell and value in use. If individual recoverable amounts cannot be determined, the assessment is performed for the relevant cash-generating unit. An impairment loss is recognised when carrying amount exceeds recoverable amount. A subsequent reversal is limited to the carrying amount that would have existed without impairment, and impairment of goodwill is not reversed. Inventories and financial assets follow their respective impairment requirements.",
  "policyIntangibleAssets": "Separately acquired intangible assets are initially measured at cost and subsequently at cost less accumulated amortisation and impairment. Internally generated expenditure, including research and development expenditure, is expensed unless it forms part of the cost of another asset that meets the applicable recognition criteria. All intangible assets have finite useful lives under IFRS for SMEs. Amortisation begins when the asset is available for use and reflects the expected pattern of consumption; if that pattern cannot be reliably determined, the straight-line method is used. Contractual rights and renewal evidence limit useful lives. Useful lives and methods are reviewed when indicators change, and assets are assessed for impairment.",
  "policyEmployeeBenefits": "Short-term employee benefits are recognised as an expense and liability as employees render the related service, at the undiscounted amount expected to be paid. Accumulating paid absences are accrued when service increases entitlement; bonuses and profit-sharing are accrued when a legal or constructive obligation exists and can be reliably estimated. Defined contribution pension costs are expensed as contributions become payable. Defined benefit arrangements, where applicable, are measured under the selected framework and the plan-specific measurement and remeasurement policy is disclosed. Termination benefits are recognised when the entity is demonstrably committed to termination or to a qualifying offer.",
  "policyProvisionsContingencies": "A provision is recognised when a past event creates a present legal or constructive obligation, an outflow of resources is probable and the amount can be reliably estimated. It is measured at the best estimate of expenditure required to settle the obligation at the reporting date, discounted where the time value of money is material. The unwinding of a discount is recognised as a finance cost. Provisions are reviewed at each reporting date and reversed when an outflow is no longer probable. Reimbursements are recognised separately only when virtually certain. Contingent liabilities are disclosed unless the possibility of outflow is remote; contingent assets are disclosed when an inflow is probable and recognised only when virtually certain.",
  "policyRelatedParties": "Related parties are identified from control, joint control, significant influence, key management and close-family relationships, including entities controlled or jointly controlled by relevant persons. A related-party transaction is a transfer of resources, services or obligations regardless of whether a price is charged. Relationships involving control are disclosed even without transactions. Transactions, outstanding balances, commitments, terms, guarantees and impairment information are disclosed by the required relationship categories. Key-management compensation is disclosed separately. An account flag identifies a disclosure source and does not change the recognition or measurement of the underlying transaction.",
  "policyForeignCurrency": "The functional currency reflects the primary economic environment in which the entity operates. Foreign-currency transactions are recorded at the spot exchange rate on the transaction date; an appropriate average is used only when it approximates actual rates. Monetary balances are translated at closing rates. Non-monetary items at historical cost retain transaction-date rates; non-monetary items at fair value use rates on the valuation date. Exchange differences are generally recognised in profit or loss unless the underlying transaction requires recognition outside profit or loss. Translation of foreign operations and the presentation currency, where different, follows the applicable requirements.",
  "policyBorrowingCosts": "Borrowing costs, including interest calculated using the effective interest method and finance charges on finance leases, are recognised as an expense in profit or loss in the period incurred. Borrowing costs are not capitalised into inventories, property, plant and equipment or other qualifying assets under IFRS for SMEs.",
  "policyShareCapitalEquity": "An instrument is classified as equity only when its contractual substance meets the definition of equity; otherwise it is classified as a liability or compound instrument as applicable. Proceeds from issuing equity instruments are recognised in equity, net of directly attributable issue costs and the related tax effect. Distributions to owners are recognised directly in equity when the obligation arises. Own equity instruments acquired are deducted from equity and no gain or loss is recognised in profit or loss on their purchase, sale, issue or cancellation. The nature and purpose of each reserve are disclosed.",
  "policyGovernmentGrants": "Government grants without specified future performance conditions are recognised in income when receivable. Grants imposing specified future performance conditions are recognised in income only when those conditions are met. Amounts received before the income recognition criteria are met are recognised as liabilities. Grants are measured at the fair value of assets received or receivable. Unfulfilled conditions, contingencies and other government assistance from which the entity has directly benefited are disclosed.",
  "policyShareBasedPayments": "Goods and services received in share-based payment transactions are recognised as assets or expenses when received, with a corresponding increase in equity for equity-settled awards or a liability for cash-settled awards. Employee equity-settled awards are measured using the applicable grant-date fair value and recognised over the vesting period, adjusted for service and non-market vesting conditions. Cash-settled liabilities are remeasured at each reporting date and settlement date, with changes in profit or loss. Valuation methods, significant inputs and the nature and extent of arrangements are disclosed.",
  "policyFinancialInstrumentsOffsetting": "Financial assets and financial liabilities are presented separately. Offsetting is applied only where the applicable standard expressly permits it and the required enforceable right and net or simultaneous settlement conditions are met. Income and expenses are not offset merely because they arise with the same counterparty. Loan balances due from and due to related parties are separately disclosed unless the offsetting criteria are satisfied.",
  "policyFinancialAssetImpairment": "At each reporting date financial assets measured at cost or amortised cost are assessed for objective evidence of impairment, including default, significant financial difficulty, concessions granted because of financial difficulty and adverse observable changes in expected collections. Significant assets are assessed individually; other balances may be grouped by similar credit risk. For assets at amortised cost, impairment is the difference between carrying amount and estimated future cash flows discounted at the original effective interest rate. Qualifying reversals are recognised in profit or loss but do not increase carrying amount beyond the amount without impairment.",
  "policyFinancialAssetsAmortisedCost": "Debt instruments meeting the basic financial instrument conditions are subsequently measured at amortised cost using the effective interest method, less impairment. The effective interest rate allocates interest, fees, transaction costs, premiums and discounts over the expected life of the instrument. Short-term receivables without a financing element are measured at undiscounted amounts expected to be received. Other instruments are assessed under the applicable fair value requirements.",
  "policyFinancialLiabilitiesAmortisedCost": "Qualifying debt liabilities are subsequently measured at amortised cost using the effective interest method. Interest expense includes the allocation of directly attributable transaction costs, fees, premiums and discounts over the expected life of the liability. Short-term payables without a financing element are measured at undiscounted settlement amounts. A liability is derecognised when the obligation is discharged, cancelled or expires; substantial modifications are assessed for extinguishment accounting.",
  "policyBiologicalAssets": "Biological assets for which fair value is readily determinable without undue cost or effort are measured at fair value less costs to sell at initial recognition and each reporting date; changes are recognised in profit or loss. Other biological assets are measured at cost less accumulated depreciation and impairment. Agricultural produce harvested from biological assets is measured at fair value less costs to sell at harvest, which becomes its cost for subsequent inventory accounting. The measurement model, activity, asset classes and relevant valuation assumptions are disclosed.",
  "policyInvestmentPropertyRecognition": "Investment property is property held to earn rentals, for capital appreciation or both, rather than for production, administration or sale in the ordinary course of business. It is initially measured at cost, including directly attributable expenditure. Mixed-use property is split when the portions can be separately accounted for; otherwise classification reflects the extent of owner occupation. Measurement after recognition follows the applicable fair value or cost requirements identified below.",
  "policyInvestmentPropertyCostModel": "Where fair value cannot be measured reliably without undue cost or effort on an ongoing basis, investment property is accounted for under the cost-depreciation-impairment requirements for property, plant and equipment. Depreciation methods, useful lives and the circumstances giving rise to the measurement basis are disclosed. If a reliable fair value measure becomes available without undue cost or effort, the property is measured under Section 16 from that date.",
  "policyInvestmentPropertyFairValueModel": "Investment property whose fair value can be measured reliably without undue cost or effort on an ongoing basis is measured at fair value at each reporting date. Changes in fair value are recognised in profit or loss. Fair value reflects market-participant assumptions and the condition and location of the property at the measurement date. Valuation methods, significant assumptions, use of an independent valuer, restrictions and contractual obligations are disclosed.",
  "policyInvestmentPropertyTransfers": "Transfers to or from investment property are made only when the property first meets, or ceases to meet, the definition of investment property. A change in management’s intention alone does not establish a change in use. Transfers and any change in measurement resulting from a change in the availability of reliable fair value information are accounted for under the relevant asset requirements and disclosed separately from additions and disposals.",
  "policyPropertyPlantEquipmentRecognition": "Property, plant and equipment is initially measured at cost when future economic benefits are probable and cost can be reliably measured. Cost includes purchase price, directly attributable costs of bringing the asset to the location and condition necessary for use, and the initial estimate of qualifying dismantling and restoration obligations. Administration costs, start-up losses and costs incurred after an asset is capable of operating as intended are expensed unless they meet separate recognition criteria.",
  "policyPropertyPlantEquipmentSubsequentExpenditure": "Replacement components and major inspections are capitalised when the recognition criteria are met; the carrying amount of the replaced component or previous inspection is derecognised. Day-to-day servicing, repairs and maintenance are expensed as incurred. Capitalisation does not continue merely because an asset has not yet operated at full capacity.",
  "policyPropertyPlantEquipmentDepreciation": "Depreciation starts when an asset is available for its intended use. Significant components with different consumption patterns are depreciated separately. Land is not depreciated. Depreciable amount is cost or revalued amount less residual value and is allocated systematically over useful life using the class-specific methods and lives disclosed in the property, plant and equipment note. Depreciation does not cease solely because an asset is idle. Changes in estimates are applied prospectively.",
  "policyPropertyPlantEquipmentUsefulLives": "Useful lives, residual values and depreciation methods reflect expected usage, physical wear, obsolescence and contractual or legal limits. They are reassessed when indicators suggest a significant change. A revision is accounted for prospectively as a change in estimate. The methods and useful lives or rates used for each class are disclosed in the property, plant and equipment note.",
  "policyPropertyPlantEquipmentDerecognition": "An item is derecognised on disposal or when no future economic benefits are expected from its use or disposal. The resulting gain or loss is the difference between net disposal proceeds and carrying amount and is recognised in profit or loss. Disposal gains are not classified as revenue from ordinary sales of goods.",
  "policyPropertyPlantEquipmentCostModel": "Classes accounted for under the cost model are carried at cost less accumulated depreciation and accumulated impairment losses. Recoverable amounts are assessed when impairment indicators exist. The measurement basis for each class is identified in the property, plant and equipment note.",
  "policyPropertyPlantEquipmentRevaluationModel": "Classes elected for revaluation are carried at fair value at the revaluation date less subsequent depreciation and impairment. Revaluations are made sufficiently regularly to avoid material differences from fair value and apply to the entire class. Increases are recognised in other comprehensive income and accumulated in a revaluation surplus, except to reverse a previous expense; decreases are expensed except to the extent of a surplus for the same asset. Valuation dates, methods and the surplus movements and restrictions are disclosed.",
  "policyPropertyPlantEquipmentAssetsUnderConstruction": "Assets under construction are measured at accumulated qualifying cost less impairment and are not depreciated until available for intended use. Completed assets are transferred to the appropriate asset class when ready for use. Contractual capital commitments and any assets pledged as security are disclosed.",
  "policyPpeRecognition": "Property, plant and equipment is initially measured at cost when future economic benefits are probable and cost can be reliably measured. Cost includes purchase price, directly attributable costs of bringing the asset to the location and condition necessary for use, and the initial estimate of qualifying dismantling and restoration obligations. Administration costs, start-up losses and costs incurred after an asset is capable of operating as intended are expensed unless they meet separate recognition criteria.",
  "policyPpeSubsequent": "Replacement components and major inspections are capitalised when the recognition criteria are met; the carrying amount of the replaced component or previous inspection is derecognised. Day-to-day servicing, repairs and maintenance are expensed as incurred. Capitalisation does not continue merely because an asset has not yet operated at full capacity.",
  "policyPpeDepreciation": "Depreciation starts when an asset is available for its intended use. Significant components with different consumption patterns are depreciated separately. Land is not depreciated. Depreciable amount is cost or revalued amount less residual value and is allocated systematically over useful life using the class-specific methods and lives disclosed in the property, plant and equipment note. Depreciation does not cease solely because an asset is idle. Changes in estimates are applied prospectively.",
  "policyPpeDerecognition": "An item is derecognised on disposal or when no future economic benefits are expected from its use or disposal. The resulting gain or loss is the difference between net disposal proceeds and carrying amount and is recognised in profit or loss. Disposal gains are not classified as revenue from ordinary sales of goods.",
  "policyLeasesGeneral": "Leases are classified as finance leases when substantially all risks and rewards incidental to ownership transfer to the lessee; all other leases are operating leases. Classification is made at inception from the substance of the arrangement, rather than its legal form, and considers ownership transfer, purchase options, lease term, present value of payments and the specialised nature of the asset.",
  "policyLeasesLessee": "At commencement of a finance lease, an asset and obligation are recognised at the lower of the fair value of the leased asset and the present value of minimum lease payments. Initial direct costs are added to the asset. Payments are allocated between finance charges and reduction of the obligation using a constant periodic rate. The asset is depreciated over its useful life or, where ownership is not reasonably certain, the shorter of useful life and lease term. Operating lease payments are expensed on a straight-line basis unless another systematic basis better reflects the benefit pattern or the applicable inflation exception applies.",
  "policyLeasesLessor": "Finance leases are recognised as receivables at the net investment in the lease. Finance income is recognised to produce a constant periodic return on that investment. Operating lease assets remain recognised according to their nature and are depreciated consistently with comparable owned assets. Operating lease income is recognised on a straight-line basis unless another systematic basis is more representative or the applicable inflation exception applies. Contingent rents are recognised when earned.",
  "policyLeasesShortTermLowValue": "All leases are assessed using the finance or operating lease classification in Section 20. The IFRS 16 exemptions for short-term leases and low-value assets do not form part of this SME classification model. Material non-cancellable operating lease commitments and finance lease obligations are disclosed using the required maturity bands.",
  "policyLeases": "Leases are classified as finance leases when substantially all risks and rewards incidental to ownership transfer to the lessee; all other leases are operating leases. Classification is made at inception from the substance of the arrangement, rather than its legal form, and considers ownership transfer, purchase options, lease term, present value of payments and the specialised nature of the asset.\n\nAt commencement of a finance lease, an asset and obligation are recognised at the lower of the fair value of the leased asset and the present value of minimum lease payments. Initial direct costs are added to the asset. Payments are allocated between finance charges and reduction of the obligation using a constant periodic rate. The asset is depreciated over its useful life or, where ownership is not reasonably certain, the shorter of useful life and lease term. Operating lease payments are expensed on a straight-line basis unless another systematic basis better reflects the benefit pattern or the applicable inflation exception applies.",
  "policyRevenueRental": "Income from operating leases is recognised on a straight-line basis over the lease term unless another systematic basis better reflects the pattern in which benefits from the asset diminish, or the applicable inflation-linked escalation exception applies. Lease incentives are included in the net consideration over the lease term. Finance lease income is recognised using a constant periodic return on the net investment.",
  "policyRevenueInterest": "Interest income is recognised using the effective interest method, including the allocation of relevant transaction costs, fees, premiums and discounts over the expected life of the financial asset. Interest on impaired assets reflects the applicable impairment measurement. Amounts collected for third parties are excluded from income.",
  "policyRevenueDividends": "Dividend income is recognised when the entity’s right to receive payment is established, the economic benefits are probable and the amount can be reliably measured. Distributions representing a recovery of the investment are assessed under the applicable investment measurement requirements.",
  "policyTaxation": "Income tax comprises current and deferred tax. Current tax is measured at the amount expected to be paid to or recovered from the tax authority using enacted or substantively enacted tax rates and laws. Deferred tax is recognised on temporary differences between carrying amounts and tax bases, subject to the specified recognition exceptions, and for unused tax losses and credits when recovery is probable. Measurement reflects the expected manner of recovery or settlement and the applicable enacted or substantively enacted rates; deferred tax is not discounted. Deferred tax assets are reviewed at each reporting date. Tax is recognised in profit or loss unless it relates to items recognised outside profit or loss. Current and deferred tax are offset only when the respective legal and settlement criteria are satisfied.",
  "policyGoodwillBusinessCombinations": "Business combinations within Section 19 are accounted for using the acquisition method. The acquirer recognises identifiable acquired assets and assumed liabilities under the applicable acquisition-date measurement requirements. Goodwill is the excess of the measured combination cost over the acquirer’s interest in the net identifiable assets; a bargain purchase is reassessed before the resulting gain is recognised. Goodwill is subsequently measured at cost less accumulated amortisation and impairment. It is amortised systematically over its estimated useful life; where that life cannot be established reliably, management’s best estimate is used within the permitted ten-year limit. Goodwill impairment losses are not reversed. Common-control transactions are assessed separately because they are outside Section 19.",
  "policyConsolidation": "The reporting scope is determined by assessing control, joint control and significant influence from substantive rights and the substance of arrangements. Consolidated financial statements include the parent and controlled subsidiaries unless a permitted exemption is applied and disclosed; intragroup balances, transactions and unrealised results are eliminated. Non-controlling interests are presented separately within consolidated equity and share in profit and comprehensive income. Separate financial statements account for investments using the permitted basis elected for each investment category. Associates and jointly controlled entities are measured using the permitted model elected for their category. The scope, significant judgements, ownership interests and measurement elections are disclosed.",
  "policyDiscontinuedOperations": "A discontinued operation is a component disposed of, or meeting the applicable held-for-sale condition, that represents a separate major line of business or geographical area, forms part of a single coordinated disposal plan for such a line or area, or is a subsidiary acquired exclusively for resale. Its post-tax result is presented separately in profit or loss with comparative information represented as required. A disposal plan does not introduce the IFRS 5 measurement model into IFRS for SMEs; relevant assets remain subject to their SME measurement requirements and impairment assessment."
};

